  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nMoist, southerly flow from the Gulf, thanks to a surface high \npressure center in the Midwest and a developing low pressure \ncenter in western South Dakota, has contributed to a low-level \nstratus layer stretching through much of the plains. This cloud \ncover will limit the diurnal heating this afternoon, keeping \nhighs in the low to mid 50s. Furthermore, the pressure gradient\nacross the region will allow breezy southerly winds to continue\nthrough today, with gusts around 35-40 MPH amidst sustained \nwinds around 20-25 MPH. \n\nThese breezy, cloudy conditions will stick around into tomorrow,\nas the low-pressure center starts to move northeast.\nTemperatures tomorrow morning will be pretty similar to this \nmorning, with lows in the mid 40s, as the cloud cover is not\nexpected to clear out till mid morning/early afternoon from west\nto east with the upper level support. These clearing skies will\nallow temperatures to increase into the 60s tomorrow, though\nlingering cloud cover may make these temperatures more difficult\nto reach. The surface winds will follow a similar suit, as \nslightly weaker westerly winds poke their head into the area \nlate morning and spread eastward through the afternoon. Peak \nwind gusts Wednesday are expected to occur mid-morning, with \ngusts up to 35-40 MPH possible. \n\nThursday may actually be the nicest day this week, as mostly\nclear skies will allow highs to climb back up into the 60s in\nbetween weather systems. Southeasterly surface winds with gusts\nup to 20-25 MPH are possible during the afternoon/evening hours,\nbut will not be nearly as strong as they are today and tomorrow. \n\nThe strong surface winds return Friday afternoon, as another\nshortwave approaches the area and spawns a low pressure center\nin the lee of the Rockies. Southerly wind gusts of 40-45 MPH\namidst sustained winds around 20-25 MPH will be possible once \nagain ahead of an approaching cold front. Precipitaiton chances\nreturn to the forecast Friday evening through Saturday with this\nsystem, with best rain chances Friday evening along and north of\nInterstate 80. By Saturday, these chances primarily rest along \nand east of Highway 183 Saturday. However, model guidance has \ncontinued to trend down on the precipitation chances with this \nsystem, as the shortwave and low pressure center now track north\nof the ara. \n\nMore seasonal temperatures will return this weekend post cold \nfront passage, with a period of dry conditions forecast Sunday. \nHowever, precipitation chances reenter the forecast for the \nstart of next work week.",
